Connection Draining in AWS — Real-Time Example

Connection Draining means:

When an EC2 instance is being removed from an AWS Load Balancer, the Load Balancer stops sending new requests to that instance but allows existing requests/connections to finish.

In modern AWS terminology, you'll usually see this as Connection Draining / Deregistration Delay.

1. Simple Example:
Suppose your architecture is:

             Users
               ↓
              ALB
               ↓
       ┌───────┼───────┐
       ↓       ↓       ↓
     EC2-1   EC2-2   EC2-3

Now EC2-2 needs to be removed.

Without connection draining:

EC2-2
  ↓
Terminate immediately
  ↓
Existing requests ❌

With connection draining:

EC2-2
  ↓
Stop accepting NEW requests
  ↓
Allow EXISTING requests to finish
  ↓
Wait for deregistration delay
  ↓
Terminate


2. Real-Time Example — File Download:
Imagine a user is downloading a 2 GB file:

User
 ↓
ALB
 ↓
EC2-2
 ↓
2 GB file

The download takes 30 seconds.

At the same time, Auto Scaling decides:

"EC2-2 should be terminated."

Without draining
EC2-2
 ↓
Terminate
 ↓
Download ❌

The user may experience a failed request.

With draining
EC2-2
 ↓
Deregister from ALB
 ↓
No NEW requests
 ↓
Existing download continues
 ↓
Download completes
 ↓
EC2 terminates

That's the purpose of connection draining.


3. New vs Existing Requests ⭐
This is the most important concept.

Suppose:

EC2-2 is draining
Existing connection
User A
   ↓
ALB
   ↓
EC2-2

It can continue.

New connection
User B
   ↓
ALB
   X
EC2-2

ALB will no longer route new requests to EC2-2.

Instead:

User B
   ↓
ALB
   ↓
EC2-1 / EC2-3

So:

Existing requests → allowed to finish
New requests      → sent elsewhere


4. Deregistration Delay
With an Application Load Balancer, the setting is called:

Deregistration delay

It determines how long the load balancer waits for existing connections to complete before considering the target fully deregistered.

The default is commonly 300 seconds (5 minutes).

You can configure the value according to your application's behavior.

Example:

Deregistration delay = 300 seconds

Conceptually:

T = 0
EC2 starts draining
      ↓
No new requests
      ↓
Existing requests continue
      ↓
T = 300 sec
      ↓
Target fully deregistered

Important: the delay is a maximum waiting period, not a guarantee that every connection will always remain open for the entire period.
